From beff9e3319eb0e66c59c9b97dfae48d6a78399ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 13:37:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: ti: omap: espresso-common: keep the TWL6032 VIO
 regulator on

A reboot from mainline never comes back on the Galaxy Tab 2: after the
bootloader's logo (or its charging screen) the screen goes dark and
nothing boots until the PMIC is power-cycled with a long press of Power.

The TWL6032 VIO SMPS is on at power-up, and the vendor kernel never
touches it. twl6032.dtsi registers it, espresso-common gives it no
constraints, so the regulator core's late cleanup turns it off ~30 s
into every boot. The system keeps running without it, but the OMAP
warm reset used for restart doesn't reset the PMIC, so VIO is still off
at the next boot, and that boot hangs. With an RTC alarm armed before
the reset, the alarm is still pending after recovery, so the kernel
never got as far as rtc-twl's probe (which disables the alarm
interrupt).

On a GT-P51xx, turning VIO back on (CFG_STATE <- ON) just before a plain
"systemctl reboot" makes every reboot come back, whether the board was
last cold- or warm-booted; with VIO left off, every reboot hangs.
Turning LDO2 and LDOLN back on, the other two regulators the late cleanup
disables, isn't needed.

Keep VIO always on.

Fixes: f768d54723d1 ("ARM: dts: ti: omap: espresso-common: Add common device tree for Samsung Galaxy Tab 2 series")
Assisted-by: LLM
---
 .../boot/dts/ti/omap/omap4-samsung-espresso-common.dtsi  | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/ti/omap/omap4-samsung-espresso-common.dtsi b/arch/arm/boot/dts/ti/omap/omap4-samsung-espresso-common.dtsi
index 06651072f000..1c10db85788f 100644
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/ti/omap/omap4-samsung-espresso-common.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/ti/omap/omap4-samsung-espresso-common.dtsi
@@ -596,6 +596,15 @@ &smps4 {
 	regulator-always-on;
 };
 
+&vio {
+	/*
+	 * No consumer is known, but if VIO is off at a warm reset, the next
+	 * boot hangs before the kernel gets far. A warm reset doesn't reset
+	 * the TWL6032, so keep it on like the vendor kernel does.
+	 */
+	regulator-always-on;
+};
+
 &ldousb {
 	regulator-min-microvolt = <3300000>;
 	regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
-- 
2.55.0

